<p>Before processing type, moisture spec or packing format, the first real decision on a basmati order is variety. 1121 and the Pusa varieties (1509 and 1401) carry most of India's basmati export trade, and they differ enough that "basmati rice" alone is not a usable specification. This guide is for importers, distributors and private-label buyers choosing between them. It covers grain length, cooked elongation, aging and processing type, how each moves the price, and what goes wrong on first orders. If you are buying for a particular country, our market guides cover ports and import rules. Here we stay on the grade question itself.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<p><strong>Reading the destination table.</strong> APEDA publishes destination shares, not dollar values per country, so the table shows each market's share of India's 2025-26 basmati exports. Saudi Arabia, Iran and Iraq together take just over 40%. The six named markets add up to about 61%, so a large part of the trade goes to many smaller buyers. 1121 has its strongest pull in the Gulf and Iraq, where visible grain length matters most on a biryani or mandi plate. Pusa grades fill much of the volume in price-sensitive and repacking channels.</p>

<h2>Why does 1121 command the highest price of the basmati grades?</h2>
<p>1121 averages 8.30-8.40mm grain length before cooking. That is the longest of the commercially traded basmati varieties, and it is prized above all for elongation after cooking: the grain extends further than competing varieties without breaking. That makes it the default choice for Middle East demand, particularly biryani, kabsa and pulao, where visible grain length is part of how the dish is presented. That performance commands a real price premium over other basmati types.</p>
<p>The name is shorthand. The full variety name is Pusa Basmati 1121, bred by the Indian Agricultural Research Institute (IARI) in New Delhi, which also bred 1509 and 1401. In trade usage, "1121" means this one variety and "Pusa" means the shorter, lower-priced varieties, mainly 1509 and 1401. Seed-to-seed maturity for 1121 runs about 135-140 days, according to APEDA's variety note.</p>

<h2>Are the Pusa grades good enough to replace 1121?</h2>
<p>1509 and 1401 run somewhat shorter, at 7.80-8.20mm average grain length. They still deliver the basmati characteristics buyers pay for: aroma, a soft cooked texture and good elongation. They sell at a lower price than 1121, which makes them the practical choice for volume buyers where cost matters more than maximum length. 1509 is a short-duration variety, around 110-120 days seed to seed. That lets it fit a different planting window from 1121 and 1401, so it can reach mills earlier in the season.</p>
<p>A newer generation of Pusa varieties is replacing the originals in the field. APEDA lists Pusa Basmati 1885 (derived from 1121), 1847 (from 1509) and 1886 (from 1401) as versions of the parent varieties with built-in resistance to bacterial leaf blight and blast. The variety note gives 1847 an 8.51mm grain with a cooked kernel length of 16.69mm, and 1886 a 7.77mm grain cooking to 15.21mm. Commercially, treat these as the parent grade they derive from. Judge each lot on measured grain length and elongation, not on the variety number printed on the offer.</p>

<h2>Does aged basmati cook better, and is the premium worth paying?</h2>
<p>Aging means holding rice for 12 or 24 months in controlled storage before it ships. It improves cooking performance in both 1121 and Pusa: the rice absorbs less water, turns out less sticky and elongates further. Aged stock carries its own premium independent of variety. Buyers after the best possible cooking result often specify 1121 and 24-month aging together and accept the combined premium. For a mid-market pack, a 12-month-aged Pusa is often a better buy than fresh-crop 1121.</p>
<p>APEDA's own description names harvesting, processing and aging alongside the growing area as the reasons basmati behaves the way it does. Aging is a specification you can put in a contract, not marketing language. State the months, and ask how the stock was held.</p>

<!-- insight:spec-to-application -->
<h2>Which processing type suits your market: raw, steam, sella or golden sella?</h2>
<p>Processing type applies to 1121 and Pusa alike and changes texture, colour, breakage and cooking behaviour whatever the variety. Raw (white) rice is milled without parboiling and gives the softest, most aromatic cooked grain. Steam rice is steamed before milling, which firms the grain and cuts breakage. Sella is fully parboiled: the grain is harder and more separate after cooking, keeps its shape in hot-holding, and is the usual food-service choice in the Gulf. Golden sella goes further in parboiling, giving a yellow-gold uncooked grain that some markets prefer on the shelf.</p>
<p>A poorly processed 1121 can cook worse than a well-processed Pusa. That is why our <a href="/products/grains-rice/basmati-rice/">basmati rice specification framework</a> treats variety, processing, grain length, moisture, broken percentage, purity and aging as seven separate parameters to agree, not one blended "quality" judgment.</p>
<p>Match the specification to where the rice is cooked, not to the variety name alone:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Gulf restaurant and catering kitchens</strong> hold cooked rice hot for hours, so grains that stay separate matter more than aroma. 1121 sella at ≤2% broken is the usual answer: the 8.30-8.40mm grain shows on a mandi or kabsa platter, and parboiling stops it turning to mush in the hot-holding tray.</li>
<li><strong>Premium retail packs</strong> are judged in the bag before they are cooked, so length, colour and broken grains are visible faults. Raw or steam 1121, ≤1% broken, 12 or 24 months aged, with a reference sample for colour.</li>
<li><strong>Repackers and price-led retail</strong> sell on cost per kilo. A Pusa grade (7.80-8.20mm) at 2% or 5% broken still clears APEDA's basmati floor of 6.61mm and an L/B ratio above 3.5 with plenty of margin, and the saving funds the packaging.</li>
</ul>
<p>Scenario: a distributor supplying both Dubai hotels and a supermarket chain does not need one compromise grade. Two lines (1121 golden sella at 2% for the kitchens, 12-month-aged raw Pusa 1509 at 1% for the shelf) usually cost less overall than putting 1121 on both.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

