<p>All basmati for export must also clear the legal floor in APEDA's contract-registration trade notice: grain longer than 6.61mm and a length-to-breadth ratio above 3.5. Both 1121 and Pusa clear it easily, so the floor matters mainly as a check on lots sold as basmati that are not.</p>
<p>For EU and US buyers, add one more line: a pesticide residue report tested against the destination's MRL (maximum residue limit) list. Residues are a separate test from grain quality and need their own clause.</p>

<!-- insight:container-economics -->
<h2>How much basmati rice fits in a 20ft or 40ft container?</h2>
<p>About 24-26 MT net in a 20ft and 26-28 MT in a 40ft. The 40ft is weight-limited, not volume-limited: rice is dense enough to reach the road and container payload limits long before the box is full. That changes the freight decision.</p>
<p>Take the midpoints, 25 MT and 27 MT. A 40ft carries about 8% more rice than a 20ft (27 ÷ 25 = 1.08). If the 40ft costs more than 1.08 times the 20ft rate on your lane, every tonne in it costs more to ship than in a 20ft. Run that test on each quote instead of assuming the bigger box is cheaper. Our MOQ is one 20ft container. Ask for both rates and divide each by its tonnage before you choose.</p>
<p>Bag size changes handling, not tonnage. A 25 MT container holds 500 bags of 50kg, 1,000 bags of 25kg or 5,000 retail packs of 5kg. Smaller packs mean more labour at loading and destuffing, and they are usually packed in master bags or cartons that take some of the weight allowance. Agree the net rice weight per container, not the bag count, on the contract.</p>
<p>Scenario: a buyer wants to trial 1121 and Pusa 1509 in one order. A split 20ft (for example 12.5 MT of each, in clearly marked bags) keeps the shipment at the one-container MOQ and gives a side-by-side test in the buyer's own market before committing to full containers of either grade.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<!-- slot:docs -->

<p>The document most specific to basmati is the APEDA Registration-cum-Allocation Certificate (RCAC). Under DGFT Notification No. 18/2015-20, basmati export is allowed only once the contract is registered with APEDA. The exporter uploads the LC, contract or proforma invoice, and the RCAC it receives is valid for shipment for 45 days. So your LC or contract should be in place before we plan the loading date. The same trade notice sets destination-specific conditions for some markets. These include pre-shipment inspection for Iran, a certificate of inspection from the Export Inspection Council or an Export Inspection Agency for EU member states and the UK, Iceland, Liechtenstein, Norway and Switzerland, and conditions for the USA, China, Indonesia and Russia. Tell us your destination and we will map the right documents to the order.</p>

<!-- price-behaviour:start -->
<!-- insight:price-mechanism -->
<h2>How the grade choices stack up in the price you're quoted</h2>
<p>Basmati pricing is layered, and each specification choice adds or removes a layer. Variety is the largest: 1121 sits above 1509 and 1401 because of its grain length and elongation. Processing adds cost with each step. Sella and golden sella involve parboiling, drying and extra handling, so they usually price differently from raw rice of the same variety. Aging adds a holding cost and ties up stock for 12 or 24 months, so 24-month material costs more than 12-month, and 12-month more than fresh crop. Within a single variety and process, the broken-percentage level is the biggest step.</p>
<p>Outside the specification, prices follow the paddy market, and the chain runs in a fixed order. Basmati is a kharif crop: the monsoon decides how much is transplanted in June and July across the GI area of Punjab, Haryana, Himachal Pradesh, Uttarakhand, Delhi, western Uttar Pradesh and parts of Jammu &amp; Kashmir. Harvest follows from October to December, and new-crop paddy arrives at the grain markets (mandis) of Punjab and Haryana. The price mills pay for that paddy sets their cost for the year, and their milled-rice export offers follow it.</p>
<p>The lead indicator to watch is new-crop paddy prices in the Punjab and Haryana mandis. 1509 matures in about 110-120 days against 135-140 for 1121, so 1509 paddy arrives first. Its price is the season's first real signal, a few weeks before 1121 arrivals confirm or reverse it. A weak monsoon or a disease year shows up there before it shows in export offers.</p>
<p>Aging adds a twist. A 24-month-aged lot was bought at the paddy price of a crop two seasons back. When the new crop prices higher, the gap between fresh-crop and aged rice narrows. When it prices lower, the gap widens. Rupee exchange rates, freight to your port and Indian export policy change on top of this. Demand from Saudi Arabia, Iran and Iraq, which together took just over 40% of India's 2025-26 basmati exports (APEDA), sets the tone for the whole market.</p>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB, CIF or CFR against your variety, processing type, aging, broken percentage, packaging and destination port. To get an accurate quote fast, tell us all six.</p>
